# THE-268 — line-ending normalisation.
#
# Dozens of tests in this repo pin file contents by
# `sha256(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`. Those digests were recorded from LF
# content. Git's `core.autocrlf` defaults to `true` on Windows, so a Windows
# clone gets CRLF in the WORKING TREE while the committed blobs stay LF —
# every line then differs by one byte and every digest pin fails at once
# (measured: 187 failures on a clean Windows checkout).
#
# The failure is loud and misleading: it presents as "the shadcn primitives
# have all drifted". They have not. If a digest fails, fix the line endings —
# never re-record the hash. A CRLF-derived digest committed here would break
# Linux and CI for everyone, which is the outcome this file exists to prevent.
#
# `eol=lf` is the load-bearing half and is NOT stylistic. Plain `text=auto`
# normalises the blob but leaves checkout to `core.eol`, which is `native` —
# i.e. still CRLF on Windows, and the digests would still fail. `eol=lf`
# forces LF in the working tree on every platform, which is what the pins read.

# Every text file: normalise to LF on commit, check out LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries — declared explicitly rather than left to `text=auto`'s heuristic.
# The heuristic only inspects the first 8000 bytes for a NUL, and a corrupted
# binary is a far worse outcome than a failing digest, so the 13 PNGs this
# repo tracks (public/icons/*, mockups/*) are pinned by extension. The rest
# are future-proofing: they match nothing today, but a font or image added
# later is protected the moment it lands.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.webm binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
